// ## 3. Manual-review notes for MISSING functions

// ### 3.1 oo_str_alloc_payload (str/str_alloc.c)

// Takes OoStr (untrusted len + data), allocates a buffer of `len`
// bytes, copies the data. The function itself doesn't have a large
// stack buffer (the allocated heap buffer is sized by the caller).
// Risk: a hostile len can pass an absurd value, but `oo_str_alloc_
// payload` is the leaf that *creates* the buffer; the caller
// specifies the size. Stack canary not strictly needed here. The
// downstream functions (oo_read_file, oo_str_concat) that *copy into
// the buffer* are protected. RECOMMENDATION: no change.

// ### 3.2 oo_env_get (fs/os/sys_env.c)

// Takes OoStr (key). Looks up via libc getenv + returns OoStr. No
// stack buffer copy. The cap-gate (oo_cap_require_env) handles the
// untrusted cap. RECOMMENDATION: no change — the function does not
// copy untrusted bytes into a stack buffer.

// ### 3.3 oo_seal + oo_open (sec/crypto/symmetric/seal.c, open.c)

// AEAD seal/open: takes OoStr args (key, nonce, plaintext, ct, etc.)
// and uses internal AEAD state buffers. These are typically heap-
// allocated (via oo_str_alloc_payload), not stack. The seal/open
// inner loops may use stack arrays for AEAD state but they're small.
// RECOMMENDATION: no change unless the AEAD inner loop can be shown
// to write beyond 8 bytes to a stack array; if so, manual review +
// add to audit list. The 2026-09-11 focused audit found no F1-style
// issue here (only the cap-system + crypto paths).

// ### 3.4 oo_audio_capture + oo_audio_init (hw/audio/oo_audio_capture.c)

// v4.6.0 audio shim (added this session). These take OoAudioBuf*
// (out param), not OoStr. The internal state is a small struct +
// a few char[] buffers for the device_name field. The stub path
// (this host) doesn't touch real audio; the USE_ALSA path is a
// future session. RECOMMENDATION: no change for stub mode. When
// USE_ALSA is enabled, re-run canary_audit.sh and add explicit
// stack-buffer copies to the high-risk list.

